data: {"result":{"_meta":{"you/usage":{"sku":"search","results_returned":5,"extraction_mode":"none"}},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"results\":{\"web\":[{\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html\",\"title\":\"asyncio — Asynchronous I/O\",\"description\":\"Hello World!: asyncio is a library to write concurrent code using the async/await syntax. asyncio is used as a foundation for multiple Python asynchronous frameworks that provide high-performance n...\",\"snippets\":[\"Explanation of the fundamentals of asyncio. ... create and manage event loops, which provide asynchronous APIs for networking, running subprocesses, handling OS signals, etc; ... Availability: not WASI. This module does not work or is not available on WebAssembly. See WebAssembly platforms for more information. ... $ python -m asyncio asyncio REPL ...\",\"Changed in version 3.13: Uses PyREPL if possible, in which case PYTHONSTARTUP is also executed. Emits audit events. ... The source code for asyncio can be found in Lib/asyncio/.\",\"asyncio is used as a foundation for multiple Python asynchronous frameworks that provide high-performance network and web-servers, database connection libraries, distributed task queues, etc.\",\"Use \\\"await\\\" directly instead of \\\"asyncio.run()\\\". Type \\\"help\\\", \\\"copyright\\\", \\\"credits\\\" or \\\"license\\\" for more information. >>> import asyncio >>> await asyncio.sleep(10, result='hello') 'hello' This REPL provides limited compatibility with PYTHON_BASIC_REPL.\"],\"thumbnail_url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.15/_images/social_previews/summary_library_asyncio_c0d82eb4.png\",\"favicon_url\":\"https://you.com/favicon?domain=docs.python.org&size=128\"},{\"url\":\"https://realpython.com/async-io-python/\",\"title\":\"Python's asyncio: A Hands-On Walkthrough – Real Python\",\"description\":\"Explore how Python asyncio works and when to use it. Follow hands-on examples to build efficient programs with coroutines and awaitable tasks.\",\"snippets\":[\"Python’s asyncio library enables you to write concurrent code using the async and await keywords. The core building blocks of async I/O in Python are awaitable objects—most often coroutines—that an event loop schedules and executes asynchronously.\",\"You should use asyncio when your application spends significant time waiting on I/O operations, such as network requests or file access, and you want to run many of these tasks concurrently without creating extra threads or processes. Through hands-on examples, you’ll gain the practical skills to write efficient Python code using asyncio that scales gracefully with increasing I/O demands.\",\"Get Your Code: Click here to get the asyncio examples you can run yourself to watch coroutines, tasks, and queues at work and compare async code with its synchronous version side by side. Take the Quiz: Test your knowledge with our interactive “Python's asyncio: A Hands-On Walkthrough” quiz.\",\"The Python standard library has offered longstanding support for these models through its multiprocessing, concurrent.futures, and threading packages. Now it’s time to add a new member to the mix. In recent years, a separate model has been more comprehensively built into CPython: asynchronous I/O, commonly called async I/O. This model is enabled through the standard library’s asyncio package and the async and await keywords.\"],\"page_age\":\"2026-11-09T05:02:26\",\"thumbnail_url\":\"https://files.realpython.com/media/A-Complete-Walkthrough-of-Pythons-Asyncio_Watermarked.5b6b9a01fdc9.jpg\",\"favicon_url\":\"https://you.com/favicon?domain=realpython.com&size=128\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/a-conceptual-overview-of-asyncio.html\",\"title\":\"A conceptual overview of asyncio — Python 3.15.2 documentation\",\"description\":\"This HOWTO article seeks to help you build a sturdy mental model of how asyncio fundamentally works, helping you understand the how and why behind the recommended patterns. You might be 